     The minimum last stake amount can be configured using `last_stake_amount_min_ratio` - which defaults to 0.5 (50%). This means that the minimum stake amount that's ever used is `stake_amount * 0.5`. This avoids very low stake amounts, that are close to the minimum tradable amount for the pair and can be refused by the exchange.
 
+#### Limiting exposure to a single underlying currency
+
+If your pairlist contains several pairs that are all priced against (or, for futures, margined in) the same underlying currency - for example several USDT-margined futures, or several coins all quoted in the same stablecoin - a large adverse move in that one currency can hit multiple open trades simultaneously, even though each trade looked fine in isolation.
+
+To cap this concentration risk, set `max_single_currency_exposure` (as a ratio between 0 and 1) in your strategy or configuration. This limits the combined stake committed, across all currently open trades, to positions sharing the same underlying (quote/settlement) currency, to that fraction of your total tradable capital.
+
+```python
+class AwesomeStrategy(IStrategy):
+    max_single_currency_exposure = 0.6
+```
+
+With `max_single_currency_exposure=0.6`, if 60% of your tradable capital is already committed to `USDT`-denominated trades, no further `USDT`-quoted trade will open (or increase) until some of that exposure is freed up. If only part of the desired stake still fits under the limit, the new trade is sized down to fit, the same way stake amounts are already adjusted for `max_stake_amount` or low balances.
+
+This setting is disabled (`None`) by default, and is enforced identically in live/dry-run trading and in backtesting, since both paths share the same stake-validation logic.
+
 #### Static stake amount
 
 The `stake_amount` configuration statically configures the amount of stake-currency your bot will use for each trade.
